# LANDDY `.lnd`
A plain-text format for knowledge that both people and AI agents can read, write, cite and review. One file renders as a web page, slides, a kanban board, a runbook or a transit-style map, and every block keeps a stable id an agent can point to.

// profile: lnd-1.1 · scope: README · suggested_views: web
// locale: en · translation: borrador redactado con IA; las ediciones es y zh-CN son traducción automática

---

@heading h-what
text: What it looks like
level: 1
@@

@code c-hello
code_lang: lnd

# Office hours
Support is open on weekdays. **Verified.**

.// profile: lnd-1.1 · scope: example · suggested_views: web

.---

.@claim a-hours
text: Support answers from 09:00 to 17:00 local time, Monday to Friday.
certainty: Verified
source: f-policy
.@@

.@source f-policy
title: Support policy, section 2
uri: https://example.org/policy
.@@
@@

A block opens with `@type id`, takes `key: value` properties, then a Markdown body, and closes with `@@`. That is most of the grammar. The full rules are in [the specification](spec/SPEC.md).

@heading h-why
text: Why another format
level: 1
@@

@features f-why
item: **Stable ids.** Every block has an id such as `a-hours`. An agent can cite it, link it, or propose a change to that block alone.
item: **Typed blocks.** `@afirmacion` is a claim with a source, `@tarea` is a task with a status, `@accion` is a runbook step with verification and rollback. Tools know what each block is without guessing from prose.
item: **Certainty is part of the text.** Claims are marked Verified, Assumption or To verify, and the renderer shows the difference.
item: **Plain text in Git.** Diffs are readable, pull requests are the review step, and nothing is locked in a database.
item: **Safe by default.** The reference renderer escapes everything, never evaluates content, and works offline with a strict Content Security Policy.
@@

@heading h-start
text: Quick start
level: 1

1. Open `renderer/render.html` in a browser. It works from disk, with no network. Drop any `.lnd` file on it, or pick one of the bundled examples.
2. Validate a file: `python3 tools/lnd_check.py examples/en/hello.lnd` (Python 3.9 or newer, no dependencies). Add `--estricto` so warnings also fail, or `--json` for CI and agents.
3. If you are an AI agent, or you are building one, read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) first.
@@

@table tb-map
title: What is in this repository

| Path | Contents |
|---|---|
| `spec/` | The format specification (profile `lnd-1`) |
| `renderer/render.html` | Reference renderer: one self-contained HTML file |
| `tools/lnd_check.py` | Validator with stable diagnostic codes |
| `examples/` | Small valid documents in English, Spanish and Chinese |
| `docs/lnd/` | The sources of these docs, written in `.lnd` |
| `docs/legal/` | Licensing notes and contribution terms |
| `docs/guias/` | Guides, starting with auditable decisions (`@decision`) |
| `docs/historias/` | Field notes, including one written by an AI agent |
| `AGENTS.md`, `llms.txt` | Guidance for AI agents |
| `BRAND_POLICY.md` | How to use the LANDDY name; `.lnd` is free to implement |
| `docs/ACCESIBILIDAD.md` | Accessibility of the renderer and the site |
@@

